Lean Rada describes a neat trick that takes advantage of the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/currentScript">document.currentScript</a> property. You can use that to drop in a tiny inline  element that replaces itself when the page loads:<pre><code>The year is &lt;script&gt;
document.currentScript.replaceWith(
  new Date().getFullYear())&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre><p>If you define a shortcut function like this:</p><pre><code>$=(...n)=&gt;document.currentScript.replaceWith(...n)
</code></pre><p>You get a tiny template language:</p><pre><code>The year is &lt;script&gt;$(new Date().getFullYear())&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
